#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119859 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2926764-dfbc-5e4e-a6b0-db24a7298a64" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector Relative To a Given Basis by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6641cc50-250b-56ad-b14a-c1124ec8c662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245216} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd41642-b280-5a7c-b432-e2ff0d315b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245311} {:answer/id #uuid "a10427df-c462-55bc-928a-4193f572fa44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245747} {:answer/id #uuid "c751a88a-3aa0-5648-a5b6-305258fb0889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246079} {:answer/id #uuid "dbad1c26-6ccc-544d-abe3-93664ba57768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a10427df-c462-55bc-928a-4193f572fa44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245747} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ec8a63e-d3c0-5582-b9c7-8a8870cbc79b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "438d91ee-b10a-5c27-8e0b-9fc621fd3a05" :question/math-academy-id "q-112208" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,2\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}+{{(\\,6\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{3}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=2\\cdot {b}_{1}+3\\cdot {b}_{2}+6\\cdot {b}_{3}\n$$\n\nand therefore \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{2}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157248 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e0441d-7176-5665-92dd-c26fbcb98df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288030} {:answer/id #uuid "09d94921-07ab-5e03-a01d-a08146f1581b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288110} {:answer/id #uuid "6fad7b2e-173a-553d-a0c3-c112c2827a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288807} {:answer/id #uuid "d6fc6f2a-51d8-5a81-9055-de9f6ce7af3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289461} {:answer/id #uuid "efe4bf53-696a-5f8e-b619-8486266ab583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00e0441d-7176-5665-92dd-c26fbcb98df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288030} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c4b747c-3e97-5b8d-86b0-f905f174bf6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21fdd56a-1bd5-5736-b8c0-5f4753ddc47e" :question/math-academy-id "q-225166" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=2v(1-u-v),\\,y=\\frac{1-v}{1+u}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2v(1-u-v),\\,\\frac{1-v}{1+u}) \\\\  & =(2v(1-0-v),\\,\\frac{1-v}{1+0}) \\\\  & =(2v(1-v),\\,1-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2v(1-v),\\,y=1-v,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=2t(1-t),\\,y=1-t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=2(1-y)(y)\\,⇒\\,x=-2{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a left-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{4}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,1)\\to (2(1)(1-0-1),\\frac{1-1}{1+0})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(0,0)\\to (2(0)(1-0-0),\\frac{1-0}{1+0})=(0,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{4}$ is an arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1).$\n\n![](images/ff/ff3c1028da35afad6c215e88863665b5bd6647d2507af2344f9f6710f0ad6373.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5011f4d0-0c40-5764-9ee2-dff49de39782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437261} {:answer/id #uuid "639601fb-cc44-52a3-bcb1-718c744fd6ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437262} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb75712-99aa-5d77-a9d1-0d236df66a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437264} {:answer/id #uuid "cd89300c-6a2b-5a80-9770-886f817b90fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437265} {:answer/id #uuid "f132c264-24c8-5558-941c-11fddb4def73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd89300c-6a2b-5a80-9770-886f817b90fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437265} :answer-field/id #uuid "c152aa95-4d94-5d9c-b5bb-30ac91e12518" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00d74228-1684-517b-8b3d-a3ad6a19bdf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94745" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x-y,$ where $x=-s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(1)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+(-1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
